1=STUDY PLOT PIT SUMMARY: Generally there is a 15cm soft slab sitting over 30cm 1F-4F+ midpack. This 
2=overlies unconsolidated mixed/old facets to ground. The Oct rain crust can still be found 15cm up from the 
3=ground surrounded by loose depth hoar 3-5mm. The failure plain at 105cm possibly has decomposed surface 
4=hoar which formed Feb 24 5-6mm. It looks quite rounded and difficult to pick out.
5=
6=Stability tests: 1 burp test, 2 comp tests.
7=1. Burp test inconclusive, soft slab fell apart before any failure plain.
8=
9=2. 2 comp tests, same results:
10=CTE1 resistant planar @ 120cm decomposing fragments 0.5mm (this occurred below the crust @ 120-122cm)
11=CTH3 resistant planar @ 105cm rounds 0.5 (possibly decomposed surface hoar 1mm)
12=CTH6 sudden collapse @ 25cm and 14cm depth hoar conglomerated 5mm (above and below oct rain crust)
13=
14=2 WEEK SUMMARY:
15=-depth range over terrain: Over the last two weeks, there has been brief periods of intense wind. This has 
16=scoured windward facing slopes and deposited snow on the leeward aspects. In the field you can still find 
17=rocky areas and minimal snow coverage to 2m in deposition areas. Spatial variability is the key phrase.
18=
19=-Tests: At the beginning of this two week period, with the warm daytime temperatures and cool nights, 
20=stability tests were in the moderate to hard range. Approximately 20cm of snow arrived March 6-10, 
21=moderate-strong West winds, and interspersed with rain on the morning of March 9 up to 2100m. Stability tests 
22=since then have been Easy-moderate in either a storm snow interface or on the basal facets. On March 6, the 
23=steep slopes behind Hilda hostel were extensively ski cut with no results. Nearby pit resulted in Moderate to 
24=Hard results in the various storm snow interfaces and also in the basal depth hoar/facets. This site is 
25=windloaded with various Pencil resistance windcrusts in the top 50cm of the snowpack making for good 
26=bridging above the F facets that are the bottom 50cm. On March 10, a 40 deg SE aspect, windloaded slope at 
27=2100m (treeline) was extensively ski cut with no reaction. The identical and lower angled sister slope was skied 
28=which subsequently released a size 2 soft slab avalanche triggered by a dog running down the slope after the 
29=skiers were at the bottom. Pits on Parkers ridge on March 13 were moderate on a windaffected site (E aspect, 
30=2230m) releasing on decomposing fragments within the recent storm snow. The same day on a sun facing 
31=slope, SE, at 2180m resulted in rutschblock 4 releasing on an interface in the storm snow appearing to be 
32=preserved stellars 50cm down.
33=-Structure: As it is today, a firm and sometimes thick slab exists in the alpine except where it has been 
34=windscoured. At treeline, it is variable with hard and soft slabs and some places have a stiff wind crust. On S 
35=and W aspects, we are seeing a sun crust developing during the warm days over the last 2 weeks especially at 
36=lower elevations starting at about 2100m. This crust is supportive in the morning and not so in the afternoon. 
37=By the roadsides, it is largely void of significant snow from rain, warm temperatures, and did not have much 
38=snow there to begin with.
39=-Snow temperatures: The temperatures over the last week have been moving towards the rounding process 
40=(i.e. -5 at the surface, 0 at the ground, HS 100cm).
41=
42=AVALANCHE OBSERVATIONS: 
43=-We have been observing 2-3 new size 2 slab and loose avalanches every day over the past 2 weeks. These 
44=are occuring on either steep windloaded gullies or unsupported slopes and appear to be associated with 
45=daytime heating. A brief spike in natural activity occurred after the storm March 6. Explosive work on March 9 
46=in which it rained in the morning, released the Parkers slabs to ground. Other low elevation targets were also 
47=releasing a size 1.5 with large bags of Nilite at the base. Higher targets were not bombed and are presently 
48=staying put.
49=
50=STABILITY SUMMARY: Stability was improving before the March 6-10 storm as we had not had 
51=much new snow up to this point, temperatures were relatively warm and nights cool all helping to settle and 
52=strengthen the snowpack. After the last storm snow has had a few days to settle, the past few days, 
53=temperatures have cooled overall which slows the settlement process. As we enter the spring the snowpack 
54=stability will be improving for the longterm but in the short term, afternoon heating/solar affects can be a 
55=potential problem as cornices and icicles are big and ready to fall. Shallow snowpack areas are potential 
56=triggers.    
